    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d65">

                <pb facs="0136" n="74" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg74"/>

                <head>No. 60.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Williamson to Mr. Evarts</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States at Central America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1877-10-20"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 20, 1877</date>. (Received November 28.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 705.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that
                    President Barrios, of Guatemala, has issued a decree guaranteeing the liberty of
                    the press, with certain restrictions.</p>
                <p>The decree is dated the 15th of October. It has not yet been published in the
                    official journal. A copy and translation of the decree will be sent you by the
                    next mail.</p>
                <p>I would only remark now that the general impression among the better classes here
                    seems to be, that President Barrios has issued the <pb facs="0137" n="75"
                        xml:id="pg75"/> decree for the purpose of entrapping some of his enemies.
                    There is but little doubt that the unfavorable comments about the killing of the
                    priest, referred to in my No. 701, have had some influence upon the action of
                    President Barrios in this matter.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. WILLIAMSON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
